      "WAEC English is an unseen paper. The comprehension is new. The summary is new. The composition prompt will not match the hostel-letter you stored in March. If your revision is a stack of finished sample answers, you have trained a search for a familiar plot. The mark scheme pays for what you can do with a strange task in a fixed time.",
      "WAEC English Language is built around unseen work. You will meet a passage you have not revised, a summary you cannot copy, and a composition prompt that will not match last year's letter about a leaking hostel. Cramming old full answers trains you to hunt for a familiar plot. The paper rewards what you can do with a new task in a fixed time.",
      "## Comprehension as a weekday habit",
      "Treat comprehension as a weekday habit. Take one short passage from an old paper or a newspaper comment page. After one careful reading, write two sentences that capture the writer's main point. Then write a third sentence that a careless reader might add but that the text never supports. Crossing out that third sentence is the real exercise: it teaches you to stay inside what is written.",
