      "Inference items are not invitations to add weather, motive, or a moral the passage never built. They ask what the writing allows a careful reader to conclude. The safe method is short: what the text says, what it does not say, and one step that still stays inside the page.",
      "An inference question is not an invitation to invent. It is a request to name a meaning that the writer has implied with evidence, without spelling it out in a topic sentence. The passage said is a quotation or a paraphrase of a line. The passage suggested is a conclusion you can defend with that line plus context. If you cannot point to the evidence, you are guessing.",
      "Train the difference with a two-column habit. Left column: words actually printed. Right column: what those words are doing. He stared at the empty bowl is printed. He was still hungry or the food was gone may be the inference, depending on the surrounding sentences. Write both columns for a week of passages. The right column is allowed only when the left column supports it.",
      "## Said versus suggested",
      "Watch the question stems. According to the passage usually wants something stated. Suggest, imply, we can infer, the writer means, and in your own words, why often want an inference. What is the meaning of a quoted word is vocabulary in context, which is a neighbour of inference but not the same: you replace the word, you do not write a new story.",
      "A marking-safe method has four steps. Read the question and circle the line or paragraph it points to. Restate the stated facts in a few words. Ask what the writer wants the reader to understand beyond those facts. Write a short answer that names that understanding and stays close to the evidence. Then stop. Extra decoration is how candidates talk themselves into a wrong inference.",
      "Length matters. Inference items are often worth one or two marks. A three-sentence speech is not safer. One clear clause usually is. If the scheme wants two points, give two short points, each tied to a different clue in the text. Do not repeat the same idea in fancier English.",
      "Stay inside the world of the passage. If the text is about a fisherman in a coastal town, do not import a television plot about pirates. If a character looked away, you may infer discomfort or guilt only if the rest of the paragraph supports that reading. If the next sentence says he was watching a bird, you inferred the wrong cause. Context after the clue matters as much as the clue. A single later sentence often cancels a dramatic reading you liked at first glance.",
      "In your own words on an inference question is a trap for lifters. Copying the sentence that contains the clue often scores zero even when you noticed the right place. Replace the key words. Keep the meaning. If the passage says the committee shelved the proposal, your inference about their attitude might be that they delayed or refused to act, not a repeat of shelved.",
      "Practise with wrong answers as well as right ones. After you write your inference, write the trap you almost fell into: the stated fact you might have copied, or the wild guess you might have added. Seeing the trap on paper makes it less attractive in the hall. Teachers can mark this quickly: evidence, inference, rejected trap.",
      "Do not use the rest of the paper to show off general knowledge. A comprehension about a health campaign is not a test of everything you know from Integrated Science. If the question asks what the writer's tone suggests, answer from the adjectives and the examples in the passage, not from a speech you prepared on public policy.",
      "Timed practice should include a full passage, not isolated sentences. Inference depends on tone across paragraphs. Sit one comprehension a week and mark only the inference and in-your-own-words items first. Those are the ones that separate candidates who can read from candidates who can hunt for a sentence that looks like the question.",
      "If two inferences seem possible, choose the one with more support, not the more dramatic one. Examiners are not scoring creativity here. They are scoring reading. A modest, evidenced answer beats a clever one that the text does not carry.",
      "Work backwards from the question when the passage feels crowded. Find the word in the question that is not a copy of a heading, then find the paragraph that carries that idea. Infer from that neighbourhood, not from the first sentence of the passage just because it is first. Many wrong inferences come from answering a different paragraph than the one the item named. If the question numbers a paragraph, refuse to answer from a different paragraph even if that other paragraph feels richer.",
      "If your inference would still be true of a different passage, you have left this one. Bring it back to a sentence that actually sits on the page, then take one careful step from that sentence only."
